1
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:07,200
let's move now to the last part we are going to 
discuss thrashing this is actually one of the  

2
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:10,080
parts that is usually a bit more complicated

3
00:00:12,240 --> 00:00:21,040
so there is this thing called thrashing is 
something that can happen during your  

4
00:00:21,040 --> 00:00:28,720
execution and it's based on the following if 
a process does not have enough pages because  

5
00:00:28,720 --> 00:00:34,800
there are not enough frames that the process can 
use then you will have a high number of page faults  

6
00:00:35,840 --> 00:00:41,360
now you know that when you have a page fault 
there is this routine to fetch the page that maybe  

7
00:00:41,360 --> 00:00:48,960
you have to swap out so when there is a page fault 
you start using the disk and that of course has an  

8
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:56,880
implication because you have a lot of input/output 
what happens is that since the process  

9
00:00:56,880 --> 00:01:02,640
now is spending more time waiting for/reading 
and writing to the disk than actually running  

10
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:08,160
it starts behaving like an IO-bound process 
rather than a CPU-bound process and its  

11
00:01:08,160 --> 00:01:16,800
CPU consumption goes down now if your operating 
system is naive and it just assumes that since the  

12
00:01:16,800 --> 00:01:23,760
process it's not using a lot of CPU then it means 
that I have free CPU I can use for other processes  

13
00:01:23,760 --> 00:01:29,280
what happens is that the scheduler will run more 
processes but these processes will also generate a  

14
00:01:29,280 --> 00:01:34,400
lot of page faults because they will be also 
fighting to get frames that are not really

15
00:01:37,120 --> 00:01:44,960
available so this degenerates because the 
more you see that the CPU is not being used  

16
00:01:45,840 --> 00:01:52,080
the more you add processes and the less the CPU 
will be used because also these processes will be  

17
00:01:52,080 --> 00:01:58,160
fighting for the same frames or the same physical 
memory that is not enough and this is what  

18
00:01:58,160 --> 00:02:06,560
thrashing is when a process needs frames and 
here we are of course in global page replacement  

19
00:02:06,560 --> 00:02:13,520
it steals from other processes then you start 
having input/output waiting because you have to

20
00:02:16,160 --> 00:02:22,880
move the processes into the queues of those 
waiting for the disk to read and write pages  

21
00:02:23,520 --> 00:02:28,000
you think that you can run more stuff because most 
of the processes are waiting rather than  

22
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:33,120
in the ready queue so you increase even more 
but this results in those news processes you  

23
00:02:33,120 --> 00:02:38,320
want to run to steal frames from the 
other processes and this degenerates  

24
00:02:40,320 --> 00:02:46,000
so this is the effect on the CPU utilization when 
you increase the degree of multi-programming in  

25
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:54,320
the beginning it gets better you use more CPU but 
if you go above a certain threshold then the CPU  

26
00:02:54,320 --> 00:03:00,160
utilization collapses and this phase here this 
part of the graph is where you are entering the  

27
00:03:00,160 --> 00:03:06,000
thrashing mode which means this might sound 
strange but it basically means that sometimes  

28
00:03:06,800 --> 00:03:18,960
if you want to use more CPU you should 
decrease the parallelism degree now  

29
00:03:22,320 --> 00:03:30,320
how do we know or how can we know that 
paging / virtual memory is working and  

30
00:03:30,320 --> 00:03:36,080
we don't end up into thrashing well we have 
this concept that we discussed before about  

31
00:03:37,280 --> 00:03:40,560
locality we said that when we run 
a process we are not really using  

32
00:03:41,200 --> 00:03:47,440
all the pages all the time at the same time but 
that during portions of time we are going to use  

33
00:03:47,440 --> 00:03:52,640
a subset of pages maybe the ones that are related 
to a certain function and then we move to another  

34
00:03:52,640 --> 00:03:59,680
function so we change locality and so on 
so if we want to avoid trashing what we need  

35
00:03:59,680 --> 00:04:06,080
to make sure is that the space we have is enough 
to maintain at the same time all the localities  

36
00:04:06,640 --> 00:04:10,960
of the processes we are trying to run it's 
not about all the space the processes need  

37
00:04:10,960 --> 00:04:18,720
it's about the space that is needed by their 
localities so this is just an example this is  

38
00:04:20,400 --> 00:04:26,880
a graph in which you have the execution time 
and then you have a small dot every time a certain  

39
00:04:26,880 --> 00:04:33,200
process is accessing some of his pages so 
what this figure is trying to show you is that  

40
00:04:33,200 --> 00:04:42,240
you can see that during this portion of time here 
pages 18 to 24 here and 30 to 34 are being used  

41
00:04:42,240 --> 00:04:47,520
so you see here this is empty so it means that 
that is not part of the current locality because  

42
00:04:47,520 --> 00:04:53,680
you don't really try to access these pages but at 
a certain point here the locality changes so now  

43
00:04:54,880 --> 00:04:59,680
for example these three pages here are 
no longer used and these pages that were  

44
00:04:59,680 --> 00:05:06,720
not used before they start being used now so 
you can see that is changing locality over time so  

45
00:05:07,360 --> 00:05:14,400
how can the operating system in practice avoid 
entering the thrashing mode one way to go  

46
00:05:14,400 --> 00:05:20,640
is to use the working set model so you 
define a window a working set window it's  

47
00:05:20,640 --> 00:05:26,000
a fixed number of page references could be 
the last 10 000 instructions for example  

48
00:05:27,520 --> 00:05:33,600
then you keep track looking only at the last 10000 
instructions at the set of pages that you are  

49
00:05:33,600 --> 00:05:40,480
trying to that is currently being used by the 
by the process and you assume that  

50
00:05:41,840 --> 00:05:46,480
the size of the locality is the size of this 
set so here you could have five pages and  

51
00:05:46,480 --> 00:05:52,000
now you could have two pages so these are two 
different locations now you can imagine that one  

52
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:57,840
key question here is how do you decide how big the 
window should be because if you make it too small  

53
00:05:58,480 --> 00:06:03,440
then you will not really encompass the entire 
locality so you might think that you have space  

54
00:06:03,440 --> 00:06:09,600
for enough to fit your locality but actually your 
locality is bigger so you don't really have it  

55
00:06:10,240 --> 00:06:14,400
on the other end if you pick it too big then 
you might encompass several localities and  

56
00:06:14,400 --> 00:06:18,560
so you might think the opposite I don't have 
enough free space because my locality is bigger  

57
00:06:18,560 --> 00:06:24,960
but actually no your locality is not that big it's 
just that the window you take it's too big and  

58
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:32,720
what you can check is the sum of all the 
localities at each point in time and if  

59
00:06:32,720 --> 00:06:40,000
this is more than the available frames then you 
are clearly entering into the thrashing mode

60
00:06:42,240 --> 00:06:48,480
so in that case you want to suspend or swap out 
some of the processes or at least one of them

61
00:06:50,880 --> 00:06:56,240
so again challenge here how do you keep track of 
the working set how do you choose the window size?

62
00:06:57,840 --> 00:07:03,520
an alternative you can use to the working set 
model it's actually to look at the page fault  

63
00:07:03,520 --> 00:07:09,120
frequency so you can define two bounds 
for the frequency with which you can  

64
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:15,120
you observe sorry page faults and you 
decide whether to allocate or remove frames  

65
00:07:15,120 --> 00:07:22,240
and on the long run whether to swap out a process 
depending on how many page faults you get so  

66
00:07:22,240 --> 00:07:28,960
as long as they stay within the given boundaries 
it is okay if they go above you can ask for more  

67
00:07:28,960 --> 00:07:35,280
frames and below you can release frames and 
when you have many that are above then you can  

68
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:44,000
suspend some of them now here you see that you 
have two different alternatives and actually  

69
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:51,360
they are connected so as always one of the 
challenges here is that you don't really know how  

70
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:58,480
to choose the window set or the specific 
number of page faults you can tolerate until  

71
00:07:58,480 --> 00:08:03,680
you actually start running your operating system 
and see what processes the user is trying to run  

72
00:08:03,680 --> 00:08:09,600
how many of them how many cores you have available 
and so on and so forth and here you can also  

73
00:08:09,600 --> 00:08:17,280
see that there is a direct relationship between 
the working set and the page fault rate because  

74
00:08:18,480 --> 00:08:25,840
what you will see over time if you just 
look at the page fault rate is that  

75
00:08:29,120 --> 00:08:36,320
usually you start observing an increasing page 
fault rate when there is a new locality because  

76
00:08:36,320 --> 00:08:41,920
the processes start accessing pages that are 
not loaded right now then at a certain point  

77
00:08:41,920 --> 00:08:48,080
this peak goes down because now all the pages of 
that locality have been requested so they have been  

78
00:08:48,080 --> 00:08:57,040
swapped in and for a certain period of time the 
the page faults will be low because the process  

79
00:08:57,040 --> 00:09:03,360
is still accessing the same set of pages that have 
been loaded yet some of them might be swapped out  

80
00:09:03,360 --> 00:09:10,720
because of other processes stealing frames but 
the number of page faults remains low then all  

81
00:09:10,720 --> 00:09:16,800
of a sudden it starts growing again because you 
know the process is entering a different locality  

82
00:09:16,800 --> 00:09:22,480
and so on so actually if you want to find 
an approximation of how big the working set  

83
00:09:23,200 --> 00:09:30,000
could be or should be to be the 
correct one then you can monitor the  

84
00:09:31,040 --> 00:09:34,720
rate with which you observe page faults 
in your operating system but as you can  

85
00:09:34,720 --> 00:09:43,520
see this now becomes complicated because of 
this relationship between the two measures

